```bash
# See the negotiated version, cipher, and certificate from the handshake
openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -tls1_3 </dev/null
# ---> shows: Protocol: TLSv1.3, Cipher: TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, peer certificate, etc.
```
```java
// In Java, the handshake runs transparently when you open an HTTPS connection
var client = java.net.http.HttpClient.newHttpClient(); // negotiates TLS automatically
var req = java.net.http.HttpRequest.newBuilder(java.net.URI.create("https://example.com")).build();
// The first request triggers ClientHello/ServerHello/key exchange under the hood.
client.send(req, java.net.http.HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
```
*You rarely code the handshake yourself — the TLS stack performs it before your first byte of data.*

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.headers(headers -> headers
        .httpStrictTransportSecurity(hsts -> hsts
            .includeSubDomains(true)
            .preload(true)
            .maxAgeInSeconds(31536000)));   // 1 year
    return http.build();
}
// Response header sent over HTTPS:
//   Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
```
*Spring Security adds HSTS by default on HTTPS responses; this customizes the directives. Browsers ignore HSTS sent over plain HTTP.*

```java
// NEVER DO THIS — trusts any certificate, re-opens man-in-the-middle
SSLContext ctx = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
ctx.init(null, new TrustManager[]{ new X509TrustManager() {
    public void checkClientTrusted(X509Certificate[] c, String a) {}
    public void checkServerTrusted(X509Certificate[] c, String a) {} // accepts EVERYTHING
    public X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() { return new X509Certificate[0]; }
}}, new SecureRandom());
// Disabling validation defeats the entire point of TLS.
```
